ion problem","source":{"current_hash_method":"tex-environment-body-comments-removed","current_statement_sha256":"sha256:2a620e6fabb5caab195b47d4ac1dd29aa2863eae254c5365bdbfe8c84ea9c19c","file":"preprints/A-classification-of-finite-Euclidean-Ramsey-configurations-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","line":74,"producer_hash_method":"atlas-whitespace-normalized-text","producer_statement_sha256":"d6ba2c759622a96311603bd61aa79683303d6293f32631fb42aba0046eeb514e","provenance":{"kind":"computed","pin":"fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb","source":"preprints/A-classification-of-finite-Euclidean-Ramsey-configurations-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex"},"sha256":"sha256:2a620e6fabb5caab195b47d4ac1dd29aa2863eae254c5365bdbfe8c84ea9c19c","url":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-classification-of-finite-Euclidean-Ramsey-configurations-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex#L74"},"statement_kind":"theorem","text":"\\label{thm:classification}\nLet $A=\\{a_1,\\ldots,a_s\\}\\subset\\R^d$ be a finite set of at least two\npoints with affine span $\\R^d$, and define $p_i,F,B,m_F$ as above.\nThen $A$ is Ramsey if and only if there is a matrix\n$P\\in\\operatorname{Mat}_{d+1}(B)$ such that\n\\begin{align}\n (p_i\\otimes1)^T P(1\\otimes p_i)&=0\n       &&(1\\le i\\le s),\\label{eq:field-evaluations}\\\\\n m_F(P_{\\alpha\\beta})&=\\delta_{\\alpha\\beta}\n       &&(1\\le\\alpha,\\beta\\le d).\\label{eq:field-spatial}\n\\end{align}\nHere the entries of $p_i\\otimes1$ and $1\\otimes p_i$ are formed\ncomponentwise. There are no separate conditions on the multiplied\nconstant row or constant column of $P$."}
